WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.170 --> 00:00:04.720
- My name is Jonathan Mannegut,

00:00:07.190 --> 00:00:12.190
AKA JT, alias Joey Knuckles,
alias, Nicholas Scratch.

00:00:14.970 --> 00:00:16.780
Nicholas Scratch 'cause
I don't accept pennies

00:00:16.780 --> 00:00:18.480
and nickels, period.

00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:23.287
Bad luck, financial deterrent,
I don't fuck with them.

00:00:29.808 --> 00:00:32.473
A funny story how I ended up
down here on Skid Row actually.

00:00:34.956 --> 00:00:36.756
I used to have a five bedroom house,

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:39.800
three cars in the driveway.

00:00:39.800 --> 00:00:42.400
I had a classic '39 that was missing...

00:00:43.730 --> 00:00:46.329
It was a classic '39 Plymouth.

00:00:46.329 --> 00:00:48.970
It was missing one door handle.

00:00:48.970 --> 00:00:52.380
Everything else was completely
original down to the straw

00:00:52.380 --> 00:00:54.180
in the seats was still the same.

00:00:55.598 --> 00:00:57.859
It was the car that my
father drove out here

00:00:57.859 --> 00:00:59.310
from Hayneville, Alabama.

00:00:59.310 --> 00:01:01.160
He came out to L.A. looking for work.

00:01:02.852 --> 00:01:04.760
And I had a house fire.

00:01:04.760 --> 00:01:07.020
Well, actually somebody broke in my house

00:01:07.020 --> 00:01:09.760
and set it on fire while
I was in Victorville

00:01:09.760 --> 00:01:11.640
visiting my kids.

00:01:11.640 --> 00:01:14.170
As I explained to you all, I have 15 kids,

00:01:14.170 --> 00:01:16.243
13 boys, two girls.

00:01:17.560 --> 00:01:20.810
And I was on my way up
to go see my son's mother

00:01:23.093 --> 00:01:26.190
and as I was getting ready
to leave, I got a call

00:01:26.190 --> 00:01:28.090
at 6 in the morning talking

00:01:28.090 --> 00:01:30.713
about it was a fire at the house.

00:01:31.619 --> 00:01:35.490
Now mind you, it is a
big, big, big, big house.

00:01:35.490 --> 00:01:38.690
So I'm thinking it's a
little bitty, something went

00:01:38.690 --> 00:01:41.530
in the kitchen or something
burned in a trash can

00:01:41.530 --> 00:01:44.590
and one wall or the curtain
or something is burnt up.

00:01:44.590 --> 00:01:45.750
I said, well, what did y'all burn?

00:01:45.750 --> 00:01:47.760
And they like, "No, the whole house."

00:01:47.760 --> 00:01:49.527
I said, the whole house?

00:01:49.527 --> 00:01:50.800
Now mind you, I'm riding the Greyhound,

00:01:50.800 --> 00:01:55.800
then I screamed so loud I
woke up everybody on the bus,

00:01:56.120 --> 00:01:58.050
'cause I'm like, how the
fuck did you burn down

00:01:58.050 --> 00:02:00.522
a $2.5 million home?

00:02:00.522 --> 00:02:02.580
How the fuck do you do that,
you know what I'm saying?

00:02:02.580 --> 00:02:05.487
And they was like, "We was gone.

00:02:05.487 --> 00:02:06.660
"We went to the club, we came home

00:02:06.660 --> 00:02:09.017
"and the fire department was here."

00:02:10.690 --> 00:02:14.673
And that was my spiral downwards.

00:02:15.510 --> 00:02:16.923
- [Mark] That was how many years ago?

00:02:18.200 --> 00:02:19.122
- 10

00:02:19.122 --> 00:02:19.955
- [Mark] 10 years ago.

00:02:19.955 --> 00:02:21.690
- 10 years ago.

00:02:21.690 --> 00:02:26.060
I went from making five
to seven grand a week

00:02:27.337 --> 00:02:29.873
(JT snaps)
to nothing, instantly.

00:02:31.370 --> 00:02:35.270
All my recording studio
equipment, burnt, crispy.

00:02:35.270 --> 00:02:36.910
- [Mark] You were making money how?

00:02:36.910 --> 00:02:40.060
- I owned a recording
studio built at my house.

00:02:40.060 --> 00:02:42.587
I used to work, me and my cousin, Binky,

00:02:42.587 --> 00:02:46.033
me and Binky Womack, we
worked on a lot of tunes.

00:02:47.380 --> 00:02:50.260
And plus, I had my own
fair share clientele.

00:02:50.260 --> 00:02:54.260
Of course, my cousin, seeing
he got the Womack last name,

00:02:54.260 --> 00:02:56.750
he had the higher echelon coming in,

00:02:56.750 --> 00:02:58.270
but he still always managed

00:02:58.270 --> 00:03:00.050
to make sure I had plenty of work.

00:03:00.050 --> 00:03:02.390
So like I said, went from making

00:03:02.390 --> 00:03:04.830
about five, seven grand a week to nothing,

00:03:04.830 --> 00:03:09.600
to a hotel voucher from
what's the people, Red Cross,

00:03:09.600 --> 00:03:13.053
to that, meaning it was
devastating because at the time,

00:03:14.630 --> 00:03:17.903
my son's mother, Ramona,
was pregnant at the time.

00:03:19.254 --> 00:03:22.720
I think she was about eight
months at the time, too.

00:03:22.720 --> 00:03:27.510
And it was a horrible,
horrible experience.

00:03:27.510 --> 00:03:31.140
It really was and it stripped
me of everything I had in me

00:03:31.140 --> 00:03:33.950
because I've never done
really anything other

00:03:33.950 --> 00:03:38.950
than graffiti, deejay
and music at that point.

00:03:40.717 --> 00:03:43.010
That was it, I had been recording music

00:03:43.010 --> 00:03:44.453
professionally since 16.

00:03:45.760 --> 00:03:50.760
So here I was now with no
studio, no equipment, no house.

00:03:56.120 --> 00:03:58.130
I ended up having to sell my classic car

00:03:59.460 --> 00:04:01.840
and I couldn't get the price I wanted.

00:04:01.840 --> 00:04:03.850
Like I said, my girl was
pregnant at the time.

00:04:03.850 --> 00:04:05.920
I couldn't have us in the
streets, so I ended up selling

00:04:05.920 --> 00:04:10.381
what should've went for
50, 60,000 for $1,000

00:04:10.381 --> 00:04:12.250
because that was the
only offer I was getting,

00:04:12.250 --> 00:04:13.163
so I took that.

00:04:15.160 --> 00:04:19.690
That money was gone in
about two weeks or so,

00:04:19.690 --> 00:04:20.683
two, three weeks.

00:04:21.899 --> 00:04:25.640
And from that, it was couch
to this, couch to that.

00:04:25.640 --> 00:04:27.263
Then eventually, we separated.

00:04:28.750 --> 00:04:33.750
I ended up in a child custody
battle type shit situation

00:04:36.390 --> 00:04:39.850
with children services on some fugazi shit

00:04:39.850 --> 00:04:41.830
that didn't have shit to do with me,

00:04:41.830 --> 00:04:45.450
everything with the mama,
nothing to do with me.

00:04:45.450 --> 00:04:46.497
That put a whole nother toll on me

00:04:46.497 --> 00:04:50.463
'cause now my kids are
living with my sister.

00:04:52.950 --> 00:04:57.120
Me and my sister haven't got
along since my father died.

00:04:57.120 --> 00:05:01.090
I've always admired my sister,
love my sister to death,

00:05:01.090 --> 00:05:02.480
did everything she ever told me

00:05:02.480 --> 00:05:04.694
'cause she was like a second mother to me,

00:05:04.694 --> 00:05:08.930
but once my father had passed away,

00:05:08.930 --> 00:05:11.807
the house that I was
living in, she was like,

00:05:11.807 --> 00:05:14.490
"We're gonna turn this into a frat house."

00:05:14.490 --> 00:05:16.057
I'm like, what about me and my kids?

00:05:16.057 --> 00:05:17.598
"Y'all can find somewhere else to go."

00:05:17.598 --> 00:05:19.427
Like, what you mean we can
find somewhere else to go?

00:05:19.427 --> 00:05:22.250
I'm like, I'm the one that
took care of this man.

00:05:22.250 --> 00:05:25.060
I watched him pissing blood
(phone ringing)

00:05:25.060 --> 00:05:27.290
and going through everything
he was going through.

00:05:27.290 --> 00:05:28.590
I ain't even worried about that phone.

00:05:28.590 --> 00:05:29.830
Let that phone ring, man.

00:05:29.830 --> 00:05:32.480
It can't be that important, man.

00:05:32.480 --> 00:05:37.480
And so, like I said, I
watched my father deteriorate.

00:05:40.400 --> 00:05:44.920
Now, this was a man who used
to could tighten car bolts

00:05:44.920 --> 00:05:49.460
on tires with his bare
hands at the grocery store

00:05:49.460 --> 00:05:52.680
and drive all the way home and
never have to touch it again.

00:05:52.680 --> 00:05:55.330
He was just always this
big buff burly dude.

00:05:55.330 --> 00:05:57.770
And it's funny because he
worked right down the street

00:05:57.770 --> 00:05:58.640
at the Greyhound.

00:05:58.640 --> 00:05:59.473
- [Mark] Oh yes.

00:06:00.620 --> 00:06:02.253
- 37 years, he worked for them.

00:06:03.299 --> 00:06:08.299
He had two years of vacation
time and one year of sick leave

00:06:10.730 --> 00:06:13.820
at that point, when you get
37, and we all begged him

00:06:13.820 --> 00:06:15.800
to take it, take it, take it.

00:06:15.800 --> 00:06:17.810
Just take the next three years off

00:06:17.810 --> 00:06:20.010
and just collect your
check and then retire,

00:06:20.940 --> 00:06:23.680
but he ain't really want it.

00:06:23.680 --> 00:06:24.733
He wanted to work.

00:06:25.823 --> 00:06:27.730
And they found the quick
ass way to fire him

00:06:27.730 --> 00:06:29.740
because my father couldn't read and write

00:06:29.740 --> 00:06:32.230
when he first came to work for Greyhound.

00:06:32.230 --> 00:06:33.850
He had to teach himself over the years.

00:06:33.850 --> 00:06:35.700
So when they computerized everything,

00:06:36.750 --> 00:06:39.150
because he had done got
old, eyes had got bad

00:06:39.150 --> 00:06:41.650
'cause my father would
weld without the mask.

00:06:41.650 --> 00:06:43.165
That was just the only way he could do it.

00:06:43.165 --> 00:06:44.338
He couldn't do it with the mask on.

00:06:44.338 --> 00:06:45.950
He had to do it with the mask off.

00:06:45.950 --> 00:06:47.753
So his eyes was damaged so bad

00:06:49.422 --> 00:06:51.063
that it wasn't much he could do.

00:06:52.710 --> 00:06:54.760
So they had to put him in a tool shed.

00:06:54.760 --> 00:06:57.020
And once they put him in
a class to teach him how

00:06:57.020 --> 00:07:00.630
to computer and work the
computer, he fucked it all up

00:07:00.630 --> 00:07:03.670
and didn't press I guess
whatever button you're supposed

00:07:03.670 --> 00:07:06.840
to press to log out that he
had passed out the material,

00:07:06.840 --> 00:07:07.673
so they fired him.

00:07:07.673 --> 00:07:09.860
Said he passed out a million
dollars worth of material

00:07:09.860 --> 00:07:12.270
and has no way to account for it.

00:07:12.270 --> 00:07:15.233
Cut him short of his pension,
everything he had coming.

00:07:16.550 --> 00:07:17.750
Broke my father's heart.

00:07:18.910 --> 00:07:23.910
So after all of this had passed,
I finally patched things up

00:07:24.320 --> 00:07:25.393
with my son's mother.

00:07:26.260 --> 00:07:27.320
I dealt with my loss.

00:07:27.320 --> 00:07:29.440
I didn't have no insurance.

00:07:29.440 --> 00:07:31.940
So it was nothing I could
do to rebuild the house.

00:07:34.462 --> 00:07:36.062
I tried to salvage what I could.

00:07:37.149 --> 00:07:39.970
And it's funny because the
only thing that really survived

00:07:39.970 --> 00:07:43.590
the fire was I had one
wall in my dining room

00:07:44.980 --> 00:07:46.423
that was all obituaries,

00:07:47.710 --> 00:07:50.423
my uncle, my great grandfather, my father.

00:07:51.900 --> 00:07:53.500
All important people in my life.

00:07:55.130 --> 00:07:57.380
That was the only thing survived.

00:07:57.380 --> 00:07:59.320
It didn't even burn.

00:07:59.320 --> 00:08:01.410
That one wall did not burn.

00:08:01.410 --> 00:08:02.910
Everything else was destroyed.

00:08:04.920 --> 00:08:09.920
And I had worked it out with
my son's mother, the new one,

00:08:12.670 --> 00:08:17.670
Ramona, and I had moved in with her.

00:08:22.250 --> 00:08:24.650
Actually, first, I moved to Victorville

00:08:24.650 --> 00:08:27.233
where I met a excellent
brother named Mark Sykes.

00:08:28.940 --> 00:08:31.160
Gave me a job out of
no motherfucking where.

00:08:31.160 --> 00:08:33.930
I just was walking down the
street and he stopped me

00:08:33.930 --> 00:08:35.453
and asked me did I want a job.

00:08:35.453 --> 00:08:37.750
I mean, like the dream shit you imagine,

00:08:37.750 --> 00:08:39.863
like nobody ever does that.

00:08:39.863 --> 00:08:44.660
And he helped me get my
little bit of confidence back.

00:08:44.660 --> 00:08:46.810
And then he even built a
recording studio up there

00:08:46.810 --> 00:08:48.610
in Victorville and I ran it for him.

00:08:49.800 --> 00:08:52.945
But that went south after so many months

00:08:52.945 --> 00:08:55.730
because of whatever technical
difficulties he was having.

00:08:55.730 --> 00:08:59.980
But I ends up moving to Riverside

00:09:02.026 --> 00:09:04.999
because I was having a small problem,

00:09:04.999 --> 00:09:06.350
not even really a problem.

00:09:06.350 --> 00:09:09.250
I had got threatened
from my other baby mama

00:09:09.250 --> 00:09:11.388
and she's no joke, you
know what I'm saying?

00:09:11.388 --> 00:09:12.221
(JT laughs)

00:09:12.221 --> 00:09:15.240
So I was slightly scared to go home

00:09:15.240 --> 00:09:18.810
and so I ended up leaving
with my other son's mother

00:09:18.810 --> 00:09:23.453
and out to Rialto and then we
ended up moving to Riverside.

00:09:24.970 --> 00:09:28.040
And I was there for
awhile up until the point

00:09:28.040 --> 00:09:31.650
where I realized that it
wasn't working with us.

00:09:31.650 --> 00:09:33.710
I was gonna make myself miserable.

00:09:33.710 --> 00:09:36.210
But this is me having my own anxiety

00:09:36.210 --> 00:09:38.380
from not being in my own house.

00:09:38.380 --> 00:09:41.960
I'm still suffering from that
loss inside, plus my sister

00:09:41.960 --> 00:09:45.003
and father had just died two
months apart from each other.

00:09:49.172 --> 00:09:51.922
I forget exactly how it happened,

00:09:55.080 --> 00:09:59.153
but I ended up back with
my son's mother, Pierre.

00:10:05.540 --> 00:10:07.273
That was holding some weight.

00:10:07.273 --> 00:10:09.540
She was really ready
to break away from me,

00:10:09.540 --> 00:10:11.390
but she had so much love for me

00:10:11.390 --> 00:10:15.480
that she wouldn't just let
me be outside like that.

00:10:15.480 --> 00:10:18.153
So I wasn't fully homeless yet.

00:10:20.620 --> 00:10:25.280
And then me and her had a
daughter, our daughter Xanobia,

00:10:25.280 --> 00:10:27.600
Xanobia Joyce Mannegut.

00:10:27.600 --> 00:10:30.377
If you ever get to see
this, you know I love you

00:10:30.377 --> 00:10:31.210
and I miss you

00:10:32.300 --> 00:10:36.033
and I wish I'd never let
your mama take you to Texas.

00:10:43.580 --> 00:10:46.480
My son's mother and my
daughter's mother explained to me

00:10:46.480 --> 00:10:49.420
that she had found a way for her

00:10:49.420 --> 00:10:52.500
to get some Section 8 housing

00:10:52.500 --> 00:10:55.710
through one of these
homeless shelters down here.

00:10:55.710 --> 00:10:57.910
Now, at this time, I'm living in Riverside

00:11:03.580 --> 00:11:06.780
because no woman really wants her son

00:11:06.780 --> 00:11:08.130
to be away from his father.

00:11:10.220 --> 00:11:13.230
If they plan on bringing
him up any kind of manly way

00:11:13.230 --> 00:11:15.690
to where he can understand
about what a man is supposed

00:11:15.690 --> 00:11:19.593
to do and not supposed to do,
proper etiquette and whatnot.

00:11:19.593 --> 00:11:23.630
And I'm staying with
her, up there with her,

00:11:23.630 --> 00:11:28.080
with my daughter's mother and
then she tells me she wants

00:11:28.080 --> 00:11:31.560
to send the baby to Texas to
stay with her mother for awhile

00:11:31.560 --> 00:11:35.450
and me and her go into this program

00:11:35.450 --> 00:11:38.050
over it was the Union
Rescue Mission at the time

00:11:38.050 --> 00:11:43.050
and get some housing and
all of this extra, extra.

00:11:43.690 --> 00:11:47.040
I really didn't understand
it and when I came down here

00:11:47.040 --> 00:11:50.670
to look because my father who
worked down here for 37 years

00:11:50.670 --> 00:11:53.070
told me he never wanted me downtown,

00:11:53.070 --> 00:11:55.570
gave me horror stories about
it, how you'll get snatched

00:11:55.570 --> 00:11:58.360
in the alley, head cut off,
this that and the other,

00:11:58.360 --> 00:11:59.193
all my life.

00:12:00.170 --> 00:12:03.580
So when I get here, I see
all these people moving

00:12:03.580 --> 00:12:05.370
on the sidewalk and I'm look at this,

00:12:05.370 --> 00:12:07.438
it's like a crowd of bums and hobos.

00:12:07.438 --> 00:12:10.270
It's like a riot, you
know what I'm saying?

00:12:10.270 --> 00:12:14.190
I'm real uncomfortable, I'm
edgy, but at the same time,

00:12:14.190 --> 00:12:16.876
I know I'm a big dude,
I can handle myself.

00:12:16.876 --> 00:12:21.325
I don't like to fight because
I tend to go overboard.

00:12:21.325 --> 00:12:25.200
So I don't like to fight
at all, but I was leery

00:12:25.200 --> 00:12:28.240
and I had my other son,
Roland, with me at the time

00:12:28.240 --> 00:12:29.290
and he was horrified.

00:12:31.928 --> 00:12:33.850
And I'm telling him, calm
down, man, calm down.

00:12:33.850 --> 00:12:35.740
Don't worry about nothing,
just stay with me.

00:12:35.740 --> 00:12:37.860
Ain't nobody gonna bother you.

00:12:37.860 --> 00:12:42.690
And I ended up, I couldn't
find her that day.

00:12:42.690 --> 00:12:46.160
So then I took him on
home, back out to Riverside

00:12:46.160 --> 00:12:48.260
and then I came back out with my daughter

00:12:48.260 --> 00:12:51.600
and we met right over by
the Union Rescue Mission

00:12:51.600 --> 00:12:55.630
and she got inside the program

00:12:55.630 --> 00:12:57.280
and all this other little stuff

00:12:57.280 --> 00:12:58.790
and then she ended up
sending my daughter out.

00:12:58.790 --> 00:13:02.010
When she sent my daughter out,
I went back out to Riverside.

00:13:02.010 --> 00:13:04.580
I would stay down here
all week and then go home

00:13:04.580 --> 00:13:08.920
on the weekend, which
eventually began to wear thin

00:13:08.920 --> 00:13:11.487
with my mother-in-law out there.

00:13:11.487 --> 00:13:13.810
And then the woman I
was with started dating

00:13:13.810 --> 00:13:16.614
a whole nother dude and da-da, da-da, da.

00:13:16.614 --> 00:13:17.447
You know how it goes.

00:13:17.447 --> 00:13:20.120
If you ain't there, somebody
else is gonna fill your shoes.

00:13:20.120 --> 00:13:24.407
So now I'm downtown permanently.

00:13:24.407 --> 00:13:26.370
And so I'm thinking temporarily

00:13:26.370 --> 00:13:28.450
it's gonna be about six weeks.

00:13:28.450 --> 00:13:31.083
Six weeks turned into seven years.

00:13:33.337 --> 00:13:35.280
And needless to say of course me

00:13:35.280 --> 00:13:36.983
and my baby mother broke up again.

00:13:38.310 --> 00:13:40.670
And anyway, she decided she was
gonna take all her anger out

00:13:40.670 --> 00:13:44.060
on me for when we originally broke up

00:13:44.060 --> 00:13:46.860
when the fire had happened,
which was three years prior.

00:13:48.730 --> 00:13:52.573
And sent my daughter out to Texas.

00:13:56.020 --> 00:13:58.780
I haven't seen her since
she was about nine months.

00:13:58.780 --> 00:14:01.940
I have a picture, though, I do believe

00:14:04.340 --> 00:14:07.089
is her currently right now.

00:14:07.089 --> 00:14:10.123
She was staying with her
grandmother and her uncle, David,

00:14:12.650 --> 00:14:15.353
David Richard Wilson.

00:14:18.590 --> 00:14:23.590
Then time goes by and
I hear from her mother

00:14:25.360 --> 00:14:28.190
that David has to take
my daughter to the doctor

00:14:28.190 --> 00:14:31.590
and she had a boil or
some shit, da-da-da-da,

00:14:31.590 --> 00:14:35.373
and then I get a call that
they took my daughter.

00:14:37.580 --> 00:14:39.310
And I'm like, who?

00:14:39.310 --> 00:14:41.590
Child services in Texas.

